videovigilance
==============

The videovigilance module of the Tryton application platform.

Installing
----------

See INSTALL

Detection service
-----------------

Each location represents a Raspberry Pi running one detection service for its cameras. In
the location form, an administration user can generate, replace or revoke its service
credential. Copy the generated key to that Raspberry's `TRYTON_API_TOKEN`. Each key only
grants access to cameras belonging to its location; replacing or revoking it does not affect
other locations. See doc/service_api.md for the API and upgrade instructions.

Service tokens are stored encrypted with `cryptography.fernet`, using the same
`[videovigilance] secret_key` as camera passwords. Keep this key with your protected backups.
The database stores only the encrypted token and a SHA-256 fingerprint for authentication;
`TRYTON_API_TOKEN` on the Raspberry remains the original token shown in the location form.

Languages
---------

Tryton interface and web UI strings use English source text, with Catalan and Spanish
translations in locale/ca.po and locale/es.po. The notices web app uses the Tryton user's
language when signed in through Tryton; otherwise it uses an available browser language.
Alarms and push notices generated by the detection service are stored in its Tryton
user's language; set that user's language before enabling the service. Existing records
keep the text they were created with.
Prompts in the static client script remain Spanish.

Access
------

The module does not put any user in its groups, so after installing it nobody sees its menu
or can use the web app until an administrator adds the users to *Videovigilància:
administració* (everything, including the movement of the cameras and the alarm switch) or to
*Videovigilància: consulta* (read only).

Testing
-------

Run the Tryton module tests with
`python -m unittest trytond.modules.videovigilance.tests.test_module`.
Detection-service tests remain separate in `service/tests`.

